#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079491} :activity/id #uuid "ffb9cf3f-a2e6-5e5c-9682-1c0e291f5434" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048410 :topic/id #uuid "9ca16548-fcfe-5fe8-900f-8d09baee1021" :topic/math-academy-id 629} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079491 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079639 :tutorial/content "Trigonometric ratios can be used to solve for a missing side of a right triangle if one side is known along with the measure of an acute angle. However, the question of which ratio to use depends on what side is given and what side is unknown. \n\n![](images/8b/8b123fed4a9a42ceb69f3a6ff68ed89f27ae42294137f0cd12a9232d329d77b0.png)\n\nIn the triangle above, we are given an angle $\\theta = 42^\\circ,$ and the length of the hypotenuse is given but the length of the side opposite is unknown.\n\nSo, we need to choose the trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite side to the hypotenuse. This is sine, and now we can just set up the ratio, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{42}^{∘} & =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we simply solve for the unknown value $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{42}^{∘} & =\\frac{x}{8} \\\\ x & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡{42}^{∘} \\\\  & =8(0.669\\,13...) \\\\  & =5.353\\,04... \\\\  & ≈5.4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest tenth.\n\nNote that we used a calculator to evaluate $\\sin(42^\\circ).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e320c0f6-5f85-56c6-a082-c880c7e97433" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2449 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d9cbed7d-509a-59f0-97ea-b1d35e68ec34" :step/math-academy-id 456177 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079492}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078551 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078257 :question/id #uuid "b4f8dd38-d62d-5940-b936-48f68e0693b8" :quest