Calculating Side Lengths of Right Triangles Using Trigonometry" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078654}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551408} :activity/id #uuid "576dc7b4-9f19-5b00-813c-ad7409e11453" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048410 :topic/id #uuid "9ca16548-fcfe-5fe8-900f-8d09baee1021" :topic/math-academy-id 629} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "720d9d1c-5ab9-50e9-b083-1d77e7c43301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550653} {:answer/id #uuid "a27513fc-52d7-5aae-8114-61b988f68c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550788} {:answer/id #uuid "a4475272-1254-5374-b9a5-1ae13bf40580" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550796} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f65878-4423-5003-bb30-a8b179c3fc21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550875} {:answer/id #uuid "f715c199-77ad-5e4d-b6a4-19176a3b13e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551031}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550788} :answer-field/id #uuid "dee662f8-4fb6-5dbb-876b-c57ec504e694" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551207}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db59e458-8e99-5a95-814f-8adc8a6a9836" :question/math-academy-id "q-122353" :question/problem "![](images/77/77242f149dc99e0641e6e095d02aeeb1fffdf764ee664cfcc02e659f9d0e03b0.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{AC}$ rounded to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the adjacent leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AC}$ hypotenuse to the given angle $∠A.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘}) & =\\frac{7}{AC} \\\\ AC\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘}) & =7 \\\\ AC & =\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =\\frac{7}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({22}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{(0.927\\,18…)} \\\\  & =7.549\\,74… \\\\  & ≈7.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} :step/id #uuid "1a29101a-51c6-5a17-9630-96d384c00f92" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551417}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551408 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551264 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093e66c5-68b2-5de8-be74-d3e1b20f43d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550392} {:answer/id #uuid "26256598-94e5-56e9-a905-4f642b7536ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550462} {:answer/id #uuid "36140525-504a-5b6d-8a94-c1d35b940038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550500} {:answer/id #uuid "51d7af68-0a6e-5e14-8481-2347a142bf70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550561} {:answer/id #uuid "5a69af37-0c06-5df1-aa78-ca7c1546903d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550589}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550589} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b7b0522-84f1-5956-962e-86aea240df5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3c476ad6-03e3-57c6-ac0c-7d3e461a358b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122322" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9ceef67f02c022065be31de7ec1b6785d270c194aee29c4d0a20707998932134.png)\n\nGiven the triangle above, find the length of $\\overset{―}{BC}$ rounded to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{BC}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠B.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) & =\\frac{BC}{12} \\\\ BC & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡({38}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =9.456\\,12… \\\\  & ≈9.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} :step/id #uuid "632f2b5c-59a9-5ba2-801e-afe343c8884d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551417 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "852f2a8b-bd2f-56d9-8a89-cf4d4abca0e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075227} {:answer/id #uuid "cde6f835-30ad-583d-9f05-e775ff6d9f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075228} {:answer/id #uuid "ecab3255-7623-59c0-9377-0f87a64b6ba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075229} {:answer/id #uuid "55c09bd7-f331-507c-824a-02b51c48d5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075230} {:answer/id #uuid "79145e40-e6ae-57b3-bebe-da7b52227cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075231}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075231} :answer-field/id #uuid "f544d748-ad7b-531b-8a34-0f57f4f9ff6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0db5629e-25c9-5953-996f-beb611bae9d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-122360" :question/problem "A triangle $△LMN$ has $m∠L={90}^{∘},$ $m∠N={37}^{∘},$ and $LM=8.$ Find the length of $\\overset{―}{LN}$ rounded to the nearest integer." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](images/38/38dcba98867c158c671b35ec033fa1ec55c8cedc67466ebe981de9fbbae314e9.png)\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LM}$ opposite to the given angle $∠N$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LN}$ adjacent$∠N.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠N) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =\\frac{8}{LN} \\\\ LN\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =8 \\\\ LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{(0.753\\,55…)} \\\\  & =10.616\\,35… \\\\  & ≈11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} :step/id #uuid "8229b54b-7fdd-521b-bccb-cd2fbf390017"}] :activity/title "Calculating Side Lengths of Right Triangles Using Trigonometry" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550348}]}
